Below you will find  

1. A brief mapping of the columns that matter for this task (and the typical conditions you will want to apply).  
2. Ten BigQuery SQL statements ‒ starting very simple and growing in complexity ‒ that lead up to the final query which returns “period / label / artwork‑count” for the three most‑frequent labels (only counting labels that appear on ≥ 500 artworks).  Each query is preceded by a short description.  

Feel free to copy / paste any of them into the BigQuery editor.

─────────────────────────────────  
Relevant columns and how they will be used
• `objects.object_id`          : key to join the two tables.  
• `objects.period`             : the historical period we must group by.  
• `vision_api_data.object_id`  : key to join.  
• `vision_api_data.labelAnnotations.description`  
  – (ARRAY<STRUCT> field) contains every Vision‑API label attached to the image; we need to UNNEST it and count distinct artworks.  

Typical filters / conditions  
• `objects.period IS NOT NULL` – ignore records without a documented period.  
• `ARRAY_LENGTH(labelAnnotations) > 0` – only images that actually have labels.  
• `HAVING artwork_cnt >= 500` – keep only “popular” labels (≥ 500 artworks).  

─────────────────────────────────  
SQL 1 – SQL 10
```sql
-- 1. Peek at distinct period values
SELECT DISTINCT period
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.objects`
WHERE period IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at a few raw Vision‑API records to see the labelAnnotations content
SELECT object_id, labelAnnotations
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data`
WHERE ARRAY_LENGTH(labelAnnotations) > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Unnest the labelAnnotations array and show one label per row
SELECT
  object_id,
  label.description AS label_desc
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data`,
UNNEST(labelAnnotations) AS label
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. How many total (object_id, label) rows do we get once we unnest?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_labeled_rows
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data`,
UNNEST(labelAnnotations) AS label;
```

```sql
-- 5. Top‑20 most common labels across ALL periods
SELECT
  label.description AS label,
  COUNT(DISTINCT object_id) AS artwork_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data`
CROSS JOIN UNNEST(labelAnnotations) AS label
GROUP BY label
ORDER BY artwork_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join labels to the objects table so we can see the period for each label hit
SELECT
  o.period,
  label.description AS label_desc
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.objects`         AS o
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data` AS v
USING (object_id)
CROSS JOIN UNNEST(v.labelAnnotations) AS label
WHERE o.period IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate: count how many artworks per (period, label)
SELECT
  o.period,
  label.description AS label,
  COUNT(DISTINCT o.object_id) AS artwork_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.objects`         AS o
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data` AS v
USING (object_id)
CROSS JOIN UNNEST(v.labelAnnotations) AS label
WHERE o.period IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY o.period, label
ORDER BY artwork_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as #7 but keep only labels that occur on 500+ artworks
SELECT
  o.period,
  label.description AS label,
  COUNT(DISTINCT o.object_id) AS artwork_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.objects`         AS o
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data` AS v
USING (object_id)
CROSS JOIN UNNEST(v.labelAnnotations) AS label
WHERE o.period IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY o.period, label
HAVING artwork_cnt >= 500
ORDER BY artwork_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Rank the labels (≥ 500 artworks) within each period
SELECT
  period,
  label,
  artwork_cnt,
  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY period ORDER BY artwork_cnt DESC) AS label_rank
FROM (
  SELECT
    o.period,
    label.description AS label,
    COUNT(DISTINCT o.object_id) AS artwork_cnt
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.objects`         AS o
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data` AS v
  USING (object_id)
  CROSS JOIN UNNEST(v.labelAnnotations) AS label
  WHERE o.period IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY o.period, label
  HAVING artwork_cnt >= 500
)
ORDER BY period, label_rank
LIMIT 100;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: top‑3 labels per period (labels must appear on 500+ artworks)
SELECT
  period,
  label,
  artwork_cnt
FROM (
  SELECT
    period,
    label,
    artwork_cnt,
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY period ORDER BY artwork_cnt DESC) AS rn
  FROM (
    SELECT
      o.period,
      label.description AS label,
      COUNT(DISTINCT o.object_id) AS artwork_cnt
    FROM `bigquery-public-data.the_met.objects`         AS o
    JOIN `bigquery-public-data.the_met.vision_api_data` AS v
    USING (object_id)
    CROSS JOIN UNNEST(v.labelAnnotations) AS label
    WHERE o.period IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY o.period, label
    HAVING artwork_cnt >= 500
  )
)
WHERE rn <= 3          -- top 3 per period
ORDER BY period, artwork_cnt DESC;
```

Running query #10 will return exactly what was requested:  

• `period` – the historical period (e.g., “Renaissance” or “Edo period”).  
• `label`  – the Vision‑API label.  
• `artwork_cnt` – how many distinct Met object IDs in that period carry that label (each label appears on at least 500 artworks).  

You now have the full workflow to obtain the answer directly from BigQuery.
